Z||000027||Will||05-25-2001||07:12 PM||||We went to the moon. End of story.<BR>The flag was wire framed to keep its shape in the lack of wind.<BR>A picture of a lit object wll be perfectly clear even if the camera is in shadow. Anyone who says otherwise has an obvious and extreme misunderstanding of the principles of optics.<BR>Laser ranging of a retro reflector left on the moon by astronauts has allowed determination of the distance to the moon to within inches, and confirmed predictions of the increase in the diameter of the moon's orbit due to tidal interactions with the earth.<P>Don't F*** with me; I'm a physicist.<P>We haven't gone back because of the cost. It costs $10,000 per pound to get something into 200 mile orbit. The moon is 250,000 miles away.<BR>There are valuable mineral resources on the moon, but until transportation becomes a LOT cheaper, they might as well be orbiting proxima centauri.||||reg||1
Z||000028||frontal lobe||05-25-2001||07:27 PM||||Well, neverfinished's original thesis included we were trying to show that russians really sucked.  Well, I think history has now decisively established that (the russian GOVERNMENT, that is).<P>As it turns out, we didn't need to go to the moon to prove it as they did a fine job of that themselves.  However, it seemed important at the time.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Taijiguy||05-25-2001||07:56 PM||||Just to throw a couple of interesting facts into this, not specifically relating, but things that have been mentioned here.<BR>Who says we aren't decendants of aliens? Think about this: All popular religions have three primary elements as a part of their doctrines. Abstinence, fasting, and silence. Think about every "alien autopsy" you've ever heard, or the common characteristics associated with aliens: No sex organs, no digestive tracts, and no vocal chords. Hmmmmmm<BR>Ken, if you were with Rockwell, you might find this interesting. The shuttle that exploded, the Challenger(?) the explosion was credited to a faulty o-ring (grommet actually) that held the ignitor in place on top of the rocket motor. The o-ring gave way, and allowed the combustion to blow the ignitor clear out of it's opening. This in turn allowed the flame of the combustion to escape from the top of the motor, effectively lighting the second stage motor while the first stage was still connected. We all know the end result. Here's the sad part: In the Navy, I was a missile technician, providing support on a ballistic missile sub for the Posiedon missiles we carried. These missiles used a GE rocket motor similar in design and manufacture to the shuttle motors. We had a special heater outfited to the tops of the rocket motors. Its purpose: to keep the little Goodrich ignitor o-ring warm. It was found out in the early stages of development of the Posiedon missile, that when those o-rings were exposed wide temperature changes, it would weaken, and give out. Thus allowing the ignitor to pop out, exposing the missiles equipment section to combustion escaping from the top of the rocket motor. Guess who supplied GE with the o-rings for the shuttle motors. Three guesses, and the first two don't count. Had there been some communication between a few people, and a heater system worth a few thousand bucks been installed, those people wouldn't have died. Sad isn't it.<BR>OK, that's my babbling for the night...<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 05-25-2001).]||||reg||4
